          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Nicole:</strong> So, what do you do if your system gets infected?</p>
<p>The first step in incident response if you are concerned that a device or system may be infected is containment. It’s important to disconnect from the internet and the network you’re on as this will prevent any more data being sent to a malware server or will stop it spreading to other computers on your network.</p>
<p>Stay offline as much as possible unless needing to download any legitimate tools to help get rid of the malware. Disconnect after the download is complete and stay offline until the malware is removed.</p>
<p>Opening a system in safe mode or safe boot allows only the minimum software and programs to load.</p>
<p>Logging in to accounts should be avoided whilst removing malware so as not to expose passwords through copy and paste function or by 'show password' because 'key logger' malware is a common component of malware. Also avoid sharing any personal details or information whilst a device is infected.</p>
<p>Check activity monitors for malicious applications especially if a suspicious update or application has been installed.</p>
<p>Run a malware scanner. These can remove most standard infections however if your antivirus software hasn't already picked this up you should try another scanner which may be able to detect more existing and emerging malware such as a heuristic antimalware solution.</p>
<p>Verify the browser homepage this will involve connecting to the internet and clear the cache, this is the temporary storage location where data is saved.</p>
<p>If malware removal is unsuccessful, it may be that the operating system may need to be reinstalled along with applications and programs from scratch. If everything is backed up it shouldn't be a problem but contacting the system suppliers help desk may be necessary.</p>
<p>This process is exactly the same for large companies and organisations but is usually handled by tools that do these steps or a dedicated incident response team.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
  
